Manual review corrections for this page's first pass.

- $z=\text{arcsin}\,t$ used an ad-hoc \text macro for an inverse
  trigonometric function; replaced with the standard \arcsin operator:
  $z=\arcsin t$.
- "$x=-ln2t$" was missing the backslash on \ln and had no grouping,
  reading as the concatenation "l n 2 t"; corrected to $x=-\ln(2t)$.
  Verified against the given check values ($x=\ln 2$, $y=\pi/4$ when
  $t=1/4$) that this is the intended expression.
- Bare prose variables that should be math: "y is the cylinder height"
  and "the y resistance is $200\Omega$" -> $y$ in both places.
- Several "$a\,\text{and}\,b$" / "$a,b,\,\text{and}\,c$" constructs fused
  an ad-hoc \text join (or a semicolon-joined inequality pair,
  "$x\ge0;y\ge0$") with bare math, mixing prose into the math span; each
  was unwrapped so "and" (or the second inequality) sits in ordinary text
  outside math, matching this file's own convention already used for the
  gas-pressure exercise (e.g. "$k=1$, $\frac{dV}{dt}=2$ cm$^{3}$/min").
  Affected: the cylinder-volume item ("$x\,\text{and}\,y$ are both
  increasing"), the frustum item's unit list ("$x=10\,\text{in}.,\,y=12\,
  \text{in.,}\,\text{and}\,z=18\,\text{in}$" -> "$x=10$ in., $y=12$ in.,
  and $z=18$ in."), the closed-box item's dimension list and matching unit
  list, the fly-temperature item ("$x\,\text{and}\,y$ are measured in
  centimeters"), the fluid-speed item's component list and inequality
  pair, and the tree-diagram item's final "$w=w(r,s),\text{and}\,t=t(r,s)$"
  (split so "and" precedes a separate $t=t(r,s)$ span).
- The frustum figure's alt-text description ("with height x, larger
  radius y, and smaller radius x") reuses $x$ for both the height and the
  smaller radius, and never mentions $z$ at all. This contradicts the
  variable assignment given earlier in the very same exercise ("$x$ is
  the radius of the smaller circle, $y$ is the radius of the larger
  circle, and $z$ is the height of the frustum"). Verified against the
  live OpenStax page that the figure caption is verbatim upstream (a
  genuine source typo, not an extraction artifact), but the internal
  contradiction with the immediately preceding sentence in the same item
  makes the intended labeling unambiguous, so corrected the caption to
  "with height $z$, larger radius $y$, and smaller radius $x$."
  Separately, that exercise's temperature-Fahrenheit line ("$T=20^\circ
  \text{F}$" alongside "temperature is expressed in kelvins") was checked
  against the live page and is also verbatim upstream; left uncorrected
  since it is not clear whether the unit label or the surrounding
  "kelvins" convention is the actual error, and the numeric value used in
  the derivative computation is unaffected either way.
- $\sqrt{u{(x,y)}^{2}+v{(x,y)}^{2}}$ had redundant braces around
  $(x,y)$; simplified to $\sqrt{u(x,y)^{2}+v(x,y)^{2}}$.
- All exercises were checked for mathematical consistency (chain-rule
  substitutions, implicit-differentiation setups, homogeneous-function
  checks); no further mathematical corrections were needed.

